 "notes": "30 stations, each on one line, in one column. No marks, keepers or light characters are printed. The only heading inside the list is the column heading 'Nos. and Names of Stations.' (line 3313), which isn't a coast, so I left coast null for every row. All rows are under the section 'IRISH COAST: AUTUMN.', so use 'Irish coast' if the field needs a value.\n\nNumbers are missing: 9, 14, 18, 22, 28, 33 and 35-37. These seem to be the stations that made no entries this autumn, so this key isn't the full station list. The full list is just above it, and line 3307 has '40. Calf Rock, Cork  Destroyed in storm, Nov. 1881.'\n\n'do.' (ditto) in rows 6 (Barrels Rock) and 10 (Kirk Bank) stands for 'Lt.-ship' from the rows above. I recorded the type as the literal 'do.' and kept the comma that comes before it in the name ('Barrels Rock,', 'Kirk Bank,'), following the style of your 'Farn, Inner,' example.\n\nRow 8 prints 'Lt.-ship.' with a trailing full stop, but row 5 has 'Lt.-ship' without one. I kept the stop as printed.\n\n'Old Head, Kinsale' is kept whole as the name (the Old Head of Kinsale). I didn't split 'Kinsale' off as a place. 'Black Rock (Mayo)': I put 'Mayo' in place and 'Black Rock' in name, because the county is there to tell it apart from other Black Rocks. Join them back if you want the printed form as one name.\n\nSpellings copied as printed: 'Kirk Bank' is Kish Bank. 'Howth Baily' is usually spelled Howth Bailey. 'Arran Island N./S.' is usually Aran. 'Innishtrahull', 'Arranmore' and 'Skelligs' are period spellings. The suffixes N., S., E., W. are part of the names (paired lights).\n\nThe day grid that follows (lines 3346 onward) has '82' on September 3rd at line 3351. There is no station 82, so it is probably a misprint for 32. That's outside the range you asked for, but worth checking when you parse the grid.",
